3
00:00:06.800 --> 00:00:10.960
[Rebecca] Today we enter the whirlwind presidency of Theodore Roosevelt.

4
00:00:10.960 --> 00:00:23.760
[Rebecca] A man of boundless physical energy, razor-sharp intellect, and fierce moral conviction, Roosevelt took the reins of a nation dominated by corporate monopolies and declared that the public interest was supreme.

5
00:00:23.760 --> 00:00:28.720
[Rebecca] Here is George with the full story of TR and the dawn of the Square Deal.

6
00:00:30.960 --> 00:00:42.080
[George] When 42-year-old Theodore Roosevelt assumed the presidency on September 14, 1901, conservative Republican Party bosses were gripped by sheer terror.

7
00:00:42.080 --> 00:00:54.880
[George] A year earlier, New York political boss Thomas Platt had engineered Roosevelt's nomination for vice president, specifically to bury the energetic young reformer in a powerless, ceremonial dead-end office.

8
00:00:54.880 --> 00:00:57.760
[George] Senator Mark Hanna had warned his colleagues.

9
00:00:57.760 --> 00:01:02.720
[George] Don't any of you realize that there's only one life between this madman and the White House.

10
00:01:02.720 --> 00:01:05.440
[George] Now, Hannah's nightmare had come true.

11
00:01:05.440 --> 00:01:09.360
[George] Pacing the floor at McKinley's funeral, Hannah groaned aloud.

12
00:01:09.360 --> 00:01:10.480
[George] Now look.

13
00:01:10.480 --> 00:01:13.840
[George] That damned cowboy is president of the United States.

14
00:01:13.840 --> 00:01:20.000
[George] Backslash and backslash and Roosevelt was unlike any man who had ever lived in the executive mansion.

15
00:01:20.000 --> 00:01:28.320
[George] A sickly, asthmatic boy from a wealthy Manhattan family, Roosevelt had built an iron constitution through sheer willpower and boxing.

16
00:01:28.320 --> 00:01:39.920
[George] He had been a Harvard scholar, a North Dakota cattle rancher, a New York City police commissioner, an assistant secretary of the Navy, and the swashbuckling hero of San Juan Hill.

17
00:01:39.920 --> 00:01:48.880
[George] He read a book a day, spoke with breathless, staccato enthusiasm, and greeted everyone with a ferocious grin of prominent white teeth.

18
00:01:48.880 --> 00:01:59.760
[George] He possessed an almost mystical connection to the American people and an absolute conviction that the President of the United States was not a passive clerk of Congress, but a steward of the people.

19
00:01:59.800 --> 00:02:05.800
[George] Bound to do whatever the national interest required unless expressly forbidden by the Constitution.

20
00:02:05.800 --> 00:02:19.320
[George] Backslash N backslash N Roosevelt's kinetic energy and boundless curiosity electrified Washington, transforming the executive mansion into a vibrant center of intellectual, cultural, and political life.

21
00:02:19.320 --> 00:02:26.360
[George] In early 1902, Roosevelt launched his opening thunderbolt against the unchecked power of corporate monopoly.

22
00:02:26.360 --> 00:02:31.720
[George] In November 1901, the three most powerful financial titans in the world, J.

23
00:02:31.720 --> 00:02:33.880
[George] Pierpont Morgan, James J.

24
00:02:33.880 --> 00:02:35.560
[George] Hill, and Edward H.

25
00:02:35.880 --> 00:02:41.800
[George] Harriman, had merged their giant Western rail systems to create the Northern Securities Company.

26
00:02:41.800 --> 00:02:51.640
[George] Capitalized at $400 million, Northern Securities created an absolute monopoly over all rail transportation from Chicago to the Pacific Ocean.

27
00:02:51.640 --> 00:02:55.960
[George] Dictating freight rates to Western farmers and wiping out competition.

28
00:02:55.960 --> 00:03:05.240
[George] Backslash N backslash N without warning Wall Street or consulting congressional bosses, President Roosevelt ordered Attorney General Philander C.

29
00:03:05.480 --> 00:03:13.000
[George] Knox to file a federal lawsuit under the long-dormant Sherman Antitrust Act to dissolve the Northern Securities Company.

30
00:03:13.000 --> 00:03:15.720
[George] Wall Street was stunned into disbelief.

31
00:03:15.720 --> 00:03:16.040
[George] J.

32
00:03:16.040 --> 00:03:16.280
[George] P.

33
00:03:16.280 --> 00:03:23.080
[George] Morgan, accustomed to treating American presidents as corporate subordinates, rushed to the White House in fury.

34
00:03:23.080 --> 00:03:30.680
[George] Morgan looked at Roosevelt and said arrogantly, if we have done anything wrong, send your man to my man and they can fix it up.

35
00:03:30.680 --> 00:03:35.800
[George] Roosevelt smiled coldly and replied, That is not the way we do things here, Mr.

36
00:03:35.800 --> 00:03:36.520
[George] Morgan.

37
00:03:36.520 --> 00:03:39.640
[George] We don't want to fix it up, we want to stop it.

38
00:03:39.640 --> 00:03:46.400
[George] When Morgan asked if the president intended to attack his other companies, Roosevelt delivered an immortal reply.

39
00:03:46.400 --> 00:03:52.320
[George] Only when we find they have done something wrong, backslash and backslash and by challenging J.P.

40
00:03:52.560 --> 00:04:02.400
[George] Morgan's railroad monopoly, Roosevelt established that no private corporate empire, no matter how vast, was above the laws of the United States.

41
00:04:02.400 --> 00:04:10.400
[George] In May 1902, a domestic catastrophe erupted that tested Roosevelt's philosophy of executive power to its limit.

42
00:04:10.400 --> 00:04:20.640
[George] 140,000 anthracite coal miners in northeastern Pennsylvania went on strike under the leadership of John Mitchell, president of the United Mine Workers.

43
00:04:20.640 --> 00:04:28.080
[George] Anthracite was the essential heating fuel for the homes, hospitals, and schools of the entire American Northeast.

44
00:04:28.080 --> 00:04:39.920
[George] The miners worked ten-hour days in dark, suffocating shafts, breathing in black coal dust and suffering constant roof cave-ins for wages that averaged less than $600 a year.

45
00:04:39.920 --> 00:04:46.080
[George] They demanded a 20% wage increase, an eight-hour day, and formal union recognition.

46
00:04:46.080 --> 00:04:50.320
[George] Backslash N backslash N the mine operators, led by George F.

47
00:04:50.320 --> 00:04:57.680
[George] Baer, president of the Philadelphia and Reading Railway, refused to speak to Mitchell or submit the dispute to arbitration.

48
00:04:57.680 --> 00:05:11.280
[George] Baer was a pious, arrogant corporate autocrat who scandalized the nation by publishing a public letter proclaiming: the rights and interests of the laboring man will be protected and cared for, not by the labor agitators.

49
00:05:11.280 --> 00:05:17.040
[George] But by the Christian men of property to whom God has given control of the property rights of the country.

50
00:05:17.040 --> 00:05:25.280
[George] As summer turned to autumn, coal supplies dwindled to nothing, prices skyrocketed from $5 to $30 a ton.

51
00:05:25.280 --> 00:05:29.480
[George] And schools in New York and Boston prepared to close for the winter.

52
00:05:29.120 --> 00:05:33.240
[George] Riots were breaking out in major cities as cold weather approached.

53
00:05:33.480 --> 00:05:44.520
[George] Backslash and backslash and the anthracite coal miners endured horrific working conditions and constant danger, making their disciplined struggle for fair wages a national moral cause.

54
00:05:44.520 --> 00:05:58.680
[George] Under the strict constitutional view of prior Gilded Age presidents, a labor dispute between private corporations and their employees was none of the federal government's business, unless troops were needed to protect corporate property from strikers.

55
00:05:58.680 --> 00:06:07.560
[George] But Theodore Roosevelt refused to let millions of American citizens freeze in their homes while arrogant coal barons played a game of corporate pride.

56
00:06:07.560 --> 00:06:18.600
[George] On October 3, 1902, Roosevelt shattered all historical precedent by summoning both the mine operators and union leaders to a face-to-face conference in Washington.

57
00:06:18.600 --> 00:06:22.760
[George] Backslash N-backslash and the meeting was electric with tension.

58
00:06:22.760 --> 00:06:33.960
[George] John Mitchell appeared quiet, dignified, and reasonable, offering to end the strike immediately if the operators would agree to let an impartial presidential commission arbitrate the dispute.

59
00:06:33.960 --> 00:06:49.160
[George] George Bear and the operators reacted with furious insolence, berating the President of the United States for daring to invite anarchists and criminals to the White House, and demanding that Roosevelt dispatch federal troops to shoot strikers and break the Union.

60
00:06:49.160 --> 00:06:55.400
[George] Roosevelt later admitted that the arrogance of the operators tested his temper almost beyond control.

61
00:06:55.400 --> 00:07:01.000
[George] There was only one person in the room who behaved like a gentleman, Roosevelt told Friends.

62
00:07:01.000 --> 00:07:03.000
[George] And that was John Mitchell.

63
00:07:03.000 --> 00:07:03.400
[George] N.

64
00:07:03.400 --> 00:07:03.640
[George] N.

65
00:07:03.640 --> 00:07:13.800
[George] Roosevelt's historic White House Conference signaled a revolutionary departure from the past, declaring that the federal government was an independent advocate for the public welfare.

66
00:07:13.800 --> 00:07:19.200
[George] When the operators stormed out of the White House, Roosevelt prepared a nuclear option.

67
00:07:19.200 --> 00:07:21.520
[George] He summoned Lieutenant General John M.

68
00:07:21.520 --> 00:07:27.360
[George] Schofield, former commander-in-chief of the United States Army, to a confidential meeting.

69
00:07:27.360 --> 00:07:38.080
[George] Roosevelt informed Schofield that if the operators did not agree to arbitration within 48 hours, the president would issue an executive order declaring a national emergency.

70
00:07:38.080 --> 00:07:43.280
[George] Schofield would lead 10,000 federal soldiers into the Pennsylvania coalfields.

71
00:07:43.280 --> 00:07:52.240
[George] Take physical control of the mines, run them as a federal receiver, and extract coal for the American people, completely dispossessing the owners.

72
00:07:52.240 --> 00:08:01.520
[George] Backslash N backslash N Roosevelt understood that this action had no statutory authority in American law, but he was completely prepared to execute it.

73
00:08:01.520 --> 00:08:08.560
[George] I knew the coal barons would cry that this was unconstitutional, Roosevelt wrote, and that I would be impeached.

74
00:08:08.560 --> 00:08:16.240
[George] But I was determined that the coal would be mined, and the people would not freeze, and let the courts decide the legality afterward.

75
00:08:16.240 --> 00:08:21.440
[George] Roosevelt dispatched Secretary of War Ilihu Root to New York to meet secretly with J.P.

76
00:08:21.440 --> 00:08:24.080
[George] Morgan aboard Morgan's yacht Corsair.

77
00:08:24.080 --> 00:08:28.480
[George] Root informed Morgan of the president's secret plan to seize the mines.

78
00:08:28.480 --> 00:08:32.880
[George] Recognizing that Roosevelt was not bluffing, Morgan stepped in.

79
00:08:32.880 --> 00:08:39.120
[George] Twisting the arms of the reluctant mine operators and forcing them to accept presidential arbitration.

80
00:08:39.120 --> 00:08:51.200
[George] Backslash N backslash and General Schofield's readiness to execute the president's orders demonstrated Roosevelt's absolute willingness to take unprecedented constitutional risks to protect the American people.

81
00:08:51.200 --> 00:08:57.360
[George] On October 23, 1902, the five-month anthracite strike officially ended.

82
00:08:57.360 --> 00:09:04.760
[George] The miners returned to the shafts, and trainloads of coal raised north just as the first winter blizzards struck New England.

83
00:09:04.760 --> 00:09:14.040
[George] The Presidential Commission awarded the miners a 10% wage increase and a nine-hour workday, while granting the operators a modest price increase.

84
00:09:14.040 --> 00:09:33.640
[George] For the first time in American history, the federal government had intervened in a labor dispute not as the strikebreaking arm of corporate capital, but as an impartial umpire defending the public interest, backslash and backslash n from this triumph, Roosevelt coined the phrase that would define his entire domestic program.

85
00:09:33.640 --> 00:09:35.160
[George] The square deal.

86
00:09:35.160 --> 00:09:41.000
[George] Speaking to an enormous crowd in Syracuse, New York, Roosevelt articulated his vision.

87
00:09:41.000 --> 00:09:43.880
[George] All I ask is a square deal for every man.

88
00:09:43.880 --> 00:09:45.480
[George] Give him a fair chance.

89
00:09:45.480 --> 00:09:50.680
[George] Do not let him exploit his fellow citizens, and do not let his fellow citizens exploit him.

90
00:09:50.680 --> 00:09:54.520
[George] We demand that big business give the people a square deal.

91
00:09:54.520 --> 00:09:58.360
[George] And we demand that labor give big business a square deal.

92
00:09:58.360 --> 00:10:03.560
[George] Roosevelt had transformed the presidency into the supreme arbiter of American society.

93
00:10:03.560 --> 00:10:15.720
[George] Backslash N backslash N the square deal redefined American conservatism by proving that government oversight of corporate monopoly was essential to preserve individual liberty and prevent socialist revolution.

94
00:10:15.720 --> 00:10:26.520
[George] The climax of Roosevelt's first term arrived on March 14, 1904, when the Supreme Court of the United States handed down its historic decision in Northern Securities Co.

95
00:10:26.680 --> 00:10:27.000
[George] v.

96
00:10:27.000 --> 00:10:28.200
[George] United States.

97
00:10:28.200 --> 00:10:36.040
[George] By a 5-4 majority, the court ruled that the railroad holding company was an illegal monopoly and ordered it dissolved.

98
00:10:36.040 --> 00:10:44.720
[George] The ruling was a seismic victory for executive authority, reversing decades of judicial decisions that had crippled the Sherman Antitrust Act.

99
00:10:44.440 --> 00:10:48.800
[George] The federal government had proven that it was more powerful than Wall Street.

100
00:10:48.960 --> 00:10:52.480
[George] Backslash and backslash and Roosevelt was jubilant.

101
00:10:52.480 --> 00:10:57.280
[George] The most powerful men in this country were held to account by the law, he roared.

102
00:10:57.280 --> 00:11:02.240
[George] We have established that the United States government is supreme over any corporation.

103
00:11:02.240 --> 00:11:15.360
[George] In November 1904, Roosevelt was re-elected in a record-breaking popular landslide, carrying every northern and western state and winning the largest popular majority in American history up to that time.

104
00:11:15.360 --> 00:11:27.280
[George] On election night, 33 years after entering the White House as an accidental president, Roosevelt smiled broadly at his wife Edith and announced, I am no longer a political accident.

105
00:11:27.280 --> 00:11:30.000
[George] The progressive era had found its champion.

106
00:11:30.000 --> 00:11:41.440
[George] Backslash and backslash and the Supreme Court's dissolution of Northern Securities permanently affirmed the federal government's legal authority to regulate and dismantle monopolistic combinations.

107
00:11:42.000 --> 00:11:50.160
[Rebecca] Theodore Roosevelt's first term fundamentally transformed the relationship between the American presidency, corporate power, and the working public.

108
00:11:50.160 --> 00:11:57.920
[Rebecca] By forging the Square Deal, he proved that democratic government possessed the moral authority to demand fairness from the mightiest monopolies.

109
